<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Barolong Chiefdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1700 CE – 1885 CE (approx.) · capital Mafikeng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">One of the oldest Tswana chiefdoms, whose branches at Thaba Nchu and Mafikeng were drawn into the struggles between Boers and Britain.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barolong">Barolong</a> are among the oldest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tswana-people">Tswana</a> groups, settled in the southern Kalahari margins and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molopo-river">Molopo</a> valley. Under the 18th-century ruler Tau they became a powerful chiefdom, but after his death they split into branches, chief among them the Ratlou, Tshidi, Seleka and Rapulana.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chiefs ruled large towns through ward headmen and the kgotla assembly, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> herding and cereal farming as the economy. During the wars of the 1820s the Seleka under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moroka-ii">Moroka</a> moved to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thaba-nchu">Thaba Nchu</a>, where they aided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a> in 1836. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/methodism">Methodist</a> missionaries had a strong influence. The Tshidi under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montshiwa">Montshiwa</a> settled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahikeng">Mafikeng</a> and resisted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> encroachment.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> statelets of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/stellaland">Stellaland</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/goshen">Goshen</a> led <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> to intervene, and in 1885 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barolong">Barolong</a> lands were divided between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-bechuanaland">British Bechuanaland</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bechuanaland-protectorate">Bechuanaland Protectorate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahikeng">Mafikeng</a> later became famous for its siege in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1700 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.53%;width:3.68%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1700 CE – 1885 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mafikeng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Tau, Moroka, Montshiwa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Tswana religion, Methodist Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Setswana</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>South Africa, Botswana</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Rolong, Barolong boo Ratshidi</dd></dl></aside></div>
